  design, then a written spec, before any code gets written.

  One thing I want to flag up front, since it shapes everything: [38;5;153mapp.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m are two different runtimes. A
  browser script can't write to a log file, and a Node process has no user session to attach a log to. "Production
  issues" also means something different in each — a browser error is only visible to you if it's shipped somewhere,
  while Node logs can just go to stdout and be collected by whatever runs the process.

  [1mThe fork:[0m which surface the logging subsystem has to serve determines whether this is a transport problem or a
  formatting problem.

  - [1mBrowser ([38;5;153mapp.js[39m) only[0m — buys you visibility into real user failures, which is the usual meaning of "production
    issues" for a login form. Costs: you need a remote sink (an endpoint to POST to), batching/failure handling, and a
    serious PII decision, since the current code logs usernames and sits next to passwords. Hard to change later: the
    wire format and the sink contract.
  - [1mNode ([38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m) only[0m — cheap and self-contained. Structured JSON to stdout, a level from an env var, done.
    Nothing to redo later; easy to extend.
  - [1mBoth, shared core[0m — one leveled logger API with two transports. Buys consistency if this repo is really one app;
    costs an abstraction that must stay runtime-neutral, and it's the option most likely to be over-built for two files
    this size.

  [1mMy recommendation: browser-only.[0m The [38;5;153mconsole[39m calls that look like real debugging pain are all in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, and
  [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m is a three-line hello-world with nothing to diagnose. Scoping to the browser also forces the PII
  question, which is the part of this change with actual consequences.
